<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.8.4: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02457</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.8.4: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1ec71a6e-4741-4cb4-9a3d-42d704cead87</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>The quadratic equation \(x^2+7x+10=0\) is in the form of \(ax^2+bx+c=0\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of \(a\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of \(b\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 7</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of \(c\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 10</para>
<para>Examine the solution method that has been started below and use it to answer parts d and e.</para>
<para>Original equation
  \(x^2+7x+10=0\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Subtract 10 from each side.
  \(x^2+7x=-10\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply each side by 4.
  \(4x^2+4(7x)=-4(10)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\).
  \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +\_\_\_\_^2=\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\)
  \((2x+\_\_\_\_)^2=\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\)
  \(2x+\_\_\_\_= \pm \sqrt{\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\)
  \(2x=\_\_\_\_\pm \sqrt{\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\)
  \(x= \_\_\_\_\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>In Step 2, what might be a good reason for multiplying each side of the equation by 4?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> You must always multiply both sides by 4 when completing the square. </item>
  <item> If the value of \(c\) is positive and you multiply by 4, it will be easier to divide the \(b\) term by 3 before you square it. </item>
  <item> Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the \(b\) term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square. </item>
  <item> Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the squared term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the squared term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Complete the unfinished steps, then <emphasis>select two</emphasis> solutions.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> -10 </item>
  <item> -5 </item>
  <item> -2 </item>
  <item> -4 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The solutions are -5 and -2.</para>
<para>Here are the unfinished steps:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\).
  \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +(7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial.
  \((2x+7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side.
  \(2x+7= \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Subtract 7 from both sides.
  \(2x=-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Solve.
  \(x=-5\), -2</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Substitute the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) into the quadratic formula, \(x=\frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2- 4ac}}{2a}\), but do not evaluate any of the expressions. Explain how this expression is related to solving \(x^2+7x+10=0\) by completing the square.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> In both forms of solving the equation, you need to list the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) to determine the solutions. </item>
  <item> Rather than evaluating at each step, the calculation is done all at once, at the end. </item>
  <item> They are related because neither one can have a negative \(c\) value. </item>
  <item> For this equation, the quadratic formula and completing the square are not the same and therefore not related. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Rather than evaluating at each step, the calculation is done all at once, at the end.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Consider the standard form of the equation \(x^2−39=0\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of \(a\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of \(b\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 0</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of \(c\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -39</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Can you use the quadratic formula to solve this equation?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Yes </item>
  <item> No </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes
  The quadratic formula works for all quadratic equations. Substituting the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) into the quadratic formula and evaluating the expression gives the solutions.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Can you solve this equation using square roots?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Yes, the solutions are \(\pm 39\). </item>
  <item> No, there is no solution. </item>
  <item> Yes, the solutions are \(\pm \sqrt{39}\). </item>
  <item> No, this equation can only be solved by the quadratic formula. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes, the solutions are \(\pm \sqrt{39}\).
  Adding 39 to both sides of the equation gives \(x^2=39\). The solutions are \(\pm \sqrt{39}\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Clare is deriving the quadratic formula by solving \(ax^2+bx+c=0\) by completing the square.</item>
</list>
<para>She arrived at this equation: \((2ax+b)^2=b^2−4ac\).</para>
<para>Choose the best description of what she needs to do to finish solving for \(x\).</para>
<list>
  <item> Subtract \(b^2\) from each side. Find the square roots of each side. Then divide each side by 2. </item>
  <item> Find the square roots of each side. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then divide each side by \(2a\). </item>
  <item> Divide each side by 2. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then find the square roots of each side. </item>
  <item> Find the square roots of each side. Divide each side by 2. Subtract \(b \) from each side. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> She needs to find the square roots of each side. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then divide each side by \(2a\).</para></section>
